WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:03.030
Why hairpins.

2
00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:05.290
Lecture one of the Manufacturing module.

3
00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:10.390
In the sizing lectures you set a slot current density.

4
00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:12.850
That number hid an assumption:

5
00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:15.920
how much of the slot is actually copper.

6
00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.650
What did you assume?

7
00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:18.450
Hold that thought.

8
00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:23.621
Here is the same motor twice, on our own parametric model.

9
00:00:23.621 --> 00:00:24.669
Not a carmaker's motor:

10
00:00:24.669 --> 00:00:27.645
our design, same core, same slots.

11
00:00:27.645 --> 00:00:30.960
One in round wire, one in rectangular bars:

12
00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:31.760
the hairpins.

13
00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:37.279
Early EVs such as the 2012 Nissan Leaf used round wire.

14
00:00:37.279 --> 00:00:41.446
The 2010 Chevrolet Volt took hairpins to the mass market;

15
00:00:41.446 --> 00:00:47.130
by 2019 the Porsche Taycan and the Volkswagen MEB drive had followed.

16
00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:51.550
You will learn when the bars win, and what they cost.

17
00:00:52.471 --> 00:00:53.271
Chapter one.

18
00:00:52.911 --> 00:00:54.121
Slot fill.

19
00:00:55.541 --> 00:00:57.943
Round wire goes into the slot at random.

20
00:00:57.943 --> 00:01:02.434
Between the circles, between enamel and liner, there is air.

21
00:01:02.434 --> 00:01:09.321
Across published machines the copper is about 0.4 to 0.5 of the slot area.

22
00:01:10.191 --> 00:01:14.002
Rectangular bars stack flat against each other and the slot wall.

23
00:01:14.002 --> 00:01:23.203
Design papers with real liners and bend tolerances report about 0.65 to 0.75.

24
00:01:23.203 --> 00:01:26.601
Reviews quote 0.8 as a ceiling, not the norm.

25
00:01:27.631 --> 00:01:29.337
Watch the counter.

26
00:01:29.337 --> 00:01:32.937
This slot has 86.8 square millimetres.

27
00:01:32.937 --> 00:01:37.374
The round-wire version's 130 wires fill 0.44 of it;

28
00:01:37.374 --> 00:01:40.201
four bars fill 0.72.

29
00:01:41.071 --> 00:01:43.555
Resistance goes as one over copper area.

30
00:01:43.555 --> 00:01:48.523
Counting bare copper, 32 square millimetres become 55.

31
00:01:48.523 --> 00:01:54.997
At the same current the DC loss falls to 0.59, 41 percent lower.

32
00:01:54.997 --> 00:01:58.691
At the same loss, 31 percent more current.

33
00:01:59.731 --> 00:02:05.204
One group redesigned a heavy-duty traction motor from random wire to hairpin, same specification:

34
00:02:05.204 --> 00:02:15.809
continuous current density 11.3 to 14.5 amps per square millimetre, peak power density 6.1

35
00:02:15.809 --> 00:02:17.961
to 7.8 kilowatts per kilogram.

36
00:02:18.741 --> 00:02:21.521
That is the first and biggest reason.

37
00:02:21.521 --> 00:02:23.331
More copper in the same iron.

38
00:02:28.485 --> 00:02:29.765
Chapter two.

39
00:02:29.765 --> 00:02:30.565
End winding.

40
00:02:32.225 --> 00:02:34.725
Copper outside the core makes no torque;

41
00:02:34.725 --> 00:02:37.445
it only adds resistance and mass.

42
00:02:37.445 --> 00:02:42.037
In one hairpin design example the end winding was up to half of the

43
00:02:42.037 --> 00:02:42.837
whole stator winding.

44
00:02:43.615 --> 00:02:45.905
A round-wire end winding is a loose bundle.

45
00:02:45.905 --> 00:02:51.382
A hairpin end winding is legs bent to defined angles, and on the crown

46
00:02:51.382 --> 00:02:53.175
side there are no joints at all.

47
00:02:53.985 --> 00:02:58.050
Reviews quote up to 50 percent shorter end turns.

48
00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:01.574
Our own model says:

49
00:03:01.574 --> 00:03:02.374
not automatically.

50
00:03:02.265 --> 00:03:07.300
On this small 24-slot core the round-wire end winding stands 32 millimetres out of

51
00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:08.507
the core;

52
00:03:08.507 --> 00:03:11.891
the hairpin crown, 51.5.

53
00:03:11.891 --> 00:03:16.510
On our 48-slot, 8-pole EV-size core the crown is 37.6:

54
00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:20.885
mainly the pole pitch, not the slot count, sets the height.

55
00:03:21.605 --> 00:03:23.685
Crown height is geometry.

56
00:03:23.685 --> 00:03:28.575
Each leg travels half the coil pitch around the bore before meeting its partner,

57
00:03:28.575 --> 00:03:34.625
and the legs of four layers must clear each other by 1.5 millimetres.

58
00:03:34.625 --> 00:03:36.505
The motor can still shrink:

59
00:03:36.505 --> 00:03:42.932
in one like-for-like redesign the stack went from 150 to 140 millimetres, because the

60
00:03:42.932 --> 00:03:44.245
slot holds more copper.

61
00:03:45.374 --> 00:03:46.174
Chapter three.

62
00:03:45.944 --> 00:03:47.154
Heat path.

63
00:03:48.474 --> 00:03:50.494
Loss is made in the copper.

64
00:03:50.494 --> 00:03:54.556
It has to cross the slot to the tooth, then the iron, the housing

65
00:03:54.556 --> 00:03:56.214
and the coolant.

66
00:03:56.214 --> 00:03:57.744
The slot is the hardest step.

67
00:03:58.294 --> 00:04:04.290
A random slot is copper, enamel, air pockets and whatever impregnant got in, modelled

68
00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:11.794
with one equivalent conductivity, measured at around 0.45 to 0.54 watts per metre kelvin.

69
00:04:12.534 --> 00:04:15.254
A hairpin slot is modelled bar by bar:

70
00:04:15.254 --> 00:04:22.343
solid copper at 401 watts per metre kelvin, separated by thin, known insulation gaps.

71
00:04:22.343 --> 00:04:27.224
The equivalent for a flat-wire slot comes out at 1.4 to 2.8.

72
00:04:27.224 --> 00:04:29.194
Roughly three to five times better.

73
00:04:29.974 --> 00:04:34.043
On our slot in FEMM, with the same 5 watts in it, the hot

74
00:04:34.043 --> 00:04:39.177
spot rises 15.4 kelvin with round wire and 10.5 with bars:

75
00:04:39.177 --> 00:04:40.684
a third less.

76
00:04:41.454 --> 00:04:43.814
Impregnation matters for both.

77
00:04:43.814 --> 00:04:49.644
In one round-wire test motor, epoxy instead of varnish raised the slot's conductivity from

78
00:04:49.644 --> 00:04:53.164
1.65 to 2.96.

79
00:04:53.164 --> 00:04:55.484
We come back to impregnation in lecture five.

80
00:04:56.504 --> 00:04:57.564
Chapter four.

81
00:04:57.564 --> 00:04:59.704
AC loss at speed.

82
00:04:59.704 --> 00:05:00.744
This is the catch.

83
00:05:01.304 --> 00:05:05.195
A solid bar does not carry alternating current evenly.

84
00:05:05.195 --> 00:05:07.850
Skin effect pushes it to the surface;

85
00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:11.355
leakage flux from neighbouring bars pushes it toward the slot opening.

86
00:05:11.355 --> 00:05:14.474
The bar nearest the opening suffers most.

87
00:05:15.414 --> 00:05:20.088
Electrical frequency is pole pairs times speed over sixty.

88
00:05:20.088 --> 00:05:24.947
An eight-pole motor at 12,000 rpm runs at 800 hertz;

89
00:05:24.947 --> 00:05:27.875
at 18,000, 1.2 kilohertz.

90
00:05:27.875 --> 00:05:29.774
Hairpin EV motors live here.

91
00:05:30.744 --> 00:05:35.688
To see it, we modelled one slot of our stator in FEMM, a free

92
00:05:35.688 --> 00:05:36.488
finite-element program.

93
00:05:37.104 --> 00:05:40.917
With four bars, at 50 hertz the current fills the copper.

94
00:05:40.917 --> 00:05:45.665
At one kilohertz it crowds toward the slot opening.

95
00:05:45.665 --> 00:05:49.983
The loss ratio K, the AC loss divided by the DC loss at the

96
00:05:49.983 --> 00:05:53.739
same current, rises to 3.45.

97
00:05:53.739 --> 00:05:57.344
Split into eight thinner bars, it drops to 1.49.

98
00:05:58.534 --> 00:06:02.028
A published study found the same trend on taller bars:

99
00:06:02.028 --> 00:06:07.804
at one kilohertz, two layers gave 11.45, eight layers 5.56.

100
00:06:08.684 --> 00:06:10.606
Round wire is not free of this.

101
00:06:10.606 --> 00:06:16.329
Strands laid at random form loops, and circulating currents dominate.

102
00:06:16.329 --> 00:06:21.991
In the same-motor redesign at one kilohertz the random winding's loss ratio was 1.91,

103
00:06:21.991 --> 00:06:25.510
the hairpin's 1.43;

104
00:06:25.510 --> 00:06:28.994
at 333 hertz both were about 1.1.

105
00:06:30.204 --> 00:06:31.910
Two more levers.

106
00:06:31.910 --> 00:06:37.423
Thinner pins near the opening cut the loss 28 percent at one kilohertz.

107
00:06:37.423 --> 00:06:38.873
And temperature:

108
00:06:38.873 --> 00:06:43.497
hot copper is more resistive, so the frequency where the loss doubles moved from

109
00:06:43.497 --> 00:06:47.974
about 650 hertz cold to about 1,200 hertz warm.

110
00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:50.580
The design rule:

111
00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:53.980
hairpins are comfortable below about one kilohertz.

112
00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:59.300
Above that you are into many thin layers or stranded conductors, and manufacturing gets

113
00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:00.100
harder.

114
00:07:04.627 --> 00:07:05.427
Chapter five.

115
00:07:05.259 --> 00:07:07.497
Production and flexibility.

116
00:07:08.857 --> 00:07:09.657
Four phases:

117
00:07:09.654 --> 00:07:13.437
form the pins, insert, twist, weld.

118
00:07:13.437 --> 00:07:16.428
Every step can be a robot or a press.

119
00:07:16.428 --> 00:07:21.849
One review estimates that at one million units a year, this costs up to

120
00:07:21.849 --> 00:07:24.327
27 percent less than random windings.

121
00:07:25.174 --> 00:07:28.390
What you pay for that is welds and tooling:

122
00:07:28.390 --> 00:07:33.717
more than 100 laser welds per stator, seven to ten pin shapes in a

123
00:07:33.717 --> 00:07:38.474
48 or 54-slot design, and tooling that fits one stator geometry only.

124
00:07:39.504 --> 00:07:41.010
Now the freedom you give up.

125
00:07:41.010 --> 00:07:43.888
One turn per coil.

126
00:07:43.888 --> 00:07:47.880
An even number of bars per slot, usually 4, 6 or 8.

127
00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:54.518
Changing the turns means changing slot count, layers or parallel paths, in steps, with

128
00:07:54.518 --> 00:07:55.318
new tooling.

129
00:07:55.954 --> 00:08:01.104
A design needing 30 or 52 turns per coil is not a hairpin design;

130
00:08:01.104 --> 00:08:02.854
that one was built in round wire.

131
00:08:03.894 --> 00:08:06.454
What you get back is certainty.

132
00:08:06.454 --> 00:08:11.254
Every bar sits where the plan says, so the voltage between neighbours is known

133
00:08:11.254 --> 00:08:13.074
and can be designed;

134
00:08:13.074 --> 00:08:18.694
one study cut conductor-to-ground voltage by 45 percent by shifting the winding plan.

135
00:08:19.634 --> 00:08:23.445
In a random winding the first and last turn of a coil can lie

136
00:08:23.445 --> 00:08:28.006
side by side and see up to 90 percent of the applied voltage on

137
00:08:28.006 --> 00:08:30.056
a 50 nanosecond edge.

138
00:08:30.056 --> 00:08:32.194
At 800 volts, that matters.

139
00:08:33.044 --> 00:08:34.224
So.

140
00:08:34.224 --> 00:08:36.434
Frozen design, high volume:

141
00:08:36.434 --> 00:08:37.494
hairpin.

142
00:08:37.494 --> 00:08:39.694
Low volume or many variants:

143
00:08:39.694 --> 00:08:42.114
round wire, on the same winding machine.

144
00:08:47.281 --> 00:08:48.181
Summary.

145
00:08:48.181 --> 00:08:48.981
Four points.

146
00:08:50.031 --> 00:08:50.861
One.

147
00:08:50.861 --> 00:08:51.761
Fill.

148
00:08:51.761 --> 00:08:57.841
On our slot, 0.44 with round wire and 0.72 with bars.

149
00:08:57.841 --> 00:09:01.731
More copper, lower DC loss, more current for the same iron.

150
00:09:04.271 --> 00:09:05.071
Two.

151
00:09:04.569 --> 00:09:05.369
Heat.

152
00:09:04.867 --> 00:09:06.721
Flat bars touch the liner;

153
00:09:06.721 --> 00:09:11.028
the slot conducts three to five times better, and our hot spot rises a

154
00:09:11.028 --> 00:09:12.421
third less.

155
00:09:12.911 --> 00:09:13.711
Three.

156
00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:16.491
The catch is AC loss past about one kilohertz.

157
00:09:16.491 --> 00:09:20.991
Thin layers, segmented pins and correct transposition keep it in check.

158
00:09:22.001 --> 00:09:22.801
Four.

159
00:09:22.261 --> 00:09:28.831
Hairpins buy automation and certainty, and pay in welds, tooling and fixed turn counts.

160
00:09:29.791 --> 00:09:31.901
That is the end of lecture one.

161
00:09:31.901 --> 00:09:33.791
Next, lecture two:

162
00:09:33.791 --> 00:09:37.281
designing a hairpin winding in the live slider app.

163
00:09:37.281 --> 00:09:40.981
Until then, open the Viewer and trace one phase to the crown.

164
00:09:42.111 --> 00:09:46.239
Motoreka is a product of UMPSA, developed at EMDLab.

165
00:09:46.239 --> 00:09:48.941
motoreka.my.

